body:
This is [inkandswitch/subduction#286](https://github.com/inkandswitch/subduction/issues/286), not a missing-bytes divergence — and the re-drive cannot fix it.

The edge advertises `Subduction.getAllHeads()` while the host advertises materialized automerge heads; the asymmetry is already documented in `collection-synchronizer.ts` above `headsOverlap`. On subduction <= 0.16.1, `heads_assuming_minimal` extends the head set with each fragment's **boundary** instead of its **head**. A boundary-less fragment carrying the newest change therefore advertises the tree at its pre-fragment loose tip indefinitely, so the two sides share **zero** heads and `headsOverlap` cannot absorb it — while both sides hold byte-identical data.

`resyncSubduction` re-opens a sync round; it does not change what `getAllHeads()` returns, so the next pass reports the same document as `different`. That is not a harmless no-op: it costs one full `syncWithAllPeers` round per phantom-diverged document per minute, for the life of the connection.

This PR's own `sync-reliability.md` records the confirming measurement — targeted `resyncSubduction` at n=80 against `halo.spec.ts` with `--retries=0`, no reduction in failure rate. That null result is exactly what #286 predicts.

**Upstream:** #286 is closed, fixed by [subduction#290](https://github.com/inkandswitch/subduction/pull/290) (merged 2026-09-08). No npm release carries it yet — 0.16.1 was published 2026-07-30, six weeks before the merge, and `pnpm-workspace.yaml` pins 0.16.0.

**Suggestion: wait for that release and add no other measure in the meantime.** Every client-side re-ask variant — plain retry, repo-wide `shareConfigChanged`, targeted `resyncSubduction`, handle eviction — works around a head-advertisement bug from the wrong side of the wire, and this PR has already measured all four as ineffective. Record the cause instead, so the next reader does not re-derive it:

```suggestion
    // A persistently `different` document here is usually NOT a missing-bytes divergence: the edge
    // advertises `Subduction.getAllHeads()` while the host advertises materialized automerge heads,
    // and on subduction <= 0.16.1 `heads_assuming_minimal` returns fragment *boundaries* instead of
    // fragment *heads* (inkandswitch/subduction#286), so a boundary-less fragment carrying the
    // newest change advertises at its pre-fragment ancestor and shares no head at all. Re-driving
    // the sync cannot change what the peer advertises — four client-side re-ask variants were
    // measured at n=80 with no effect — so this waits on the upstream fix
    // (inkandswitch/subduction#290, unreleased as of 0.16.1) rather than adding a workaround.
    if (different.length > 0 && passes >= NON_CONVERGENCE_WARN_THRESHOLD) {
      log.verbose('documents reported different by collection sync', { collectionId, peerId, passes, different });
    }
```

